# Lesson 4, new exercise: nothing to lose again. West leads a low heart; dummy's K6 must go up, and then nine tricks run without giving up the lead.
title: Up with the king
id: L04-17
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.3
student: S
board: 87
dealer: S
vulnerable: both

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  Q85    K6     KQJ84  763
  E  7632   QT7    9653   AT
  S  AK4    953    A72    KQJ5
  W  JT9    AJ842  T      9842

auction:
  S 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 17 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner raised to game. You are declarer, because you
  bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 10-11
  spades: 3 = The ace, king and queen.
  hearts: 0 = Dummy's king wins only if West, who led the suit, has the ace. Fingers crossed.
  diamonds: 5 = Dummy's long suit. Cash your ace first, the high card from the short side.
  clubs: 2-3 = Two or three, once the ace is gone. But you can't afford to give up the lead: the defense would run the hearts. Your real tricks are five diamonds, three spades and, with luck, the ♥K.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: West led a low heart. Who might have the ♥A?
play:
  1   W H4  N HK*  E H7  S H3
      HK key: Nothing to lose. If West has the ace, the king wins now. If East has it, the king was never going to win a trick.
      HK not H6: Did you think about playing the king? If you play low, East plays a high heart, and later the ace captures your king. If West has the ace, the king wins right now.
  2   N D4  E D3  S DA  W DT
      note: With the ♥K in, you have nine tricks without giving up the lead: three spades, one heart and five diamonds. Leave the clubs alone. Start the diamonds with your ace, the high card from the short side.
  3   S D2  W C2  N DK  E D5
      note: Over to dummy's diamonds. West shows out, but dummy's diamonds are still good: East has only four.
  4   N DQ  E D6  S D7  W C4
      note: Keep cashing diamonds.
  5   N DJ  E D9  S H5  W C8
      note: That's the last of East's diamonds.
  6   N D8  E S2  S H9  W C9
      note: Dummy's fifth diamond.
  7   N S5  E S3  S SK  W S9
      note: Now the spades.
  8   S SA  W ST  N S8  E S6
  9   S S4  W SJ  N SQ  E S7
      note: That's nine tricks.
  10  N C3  E CA  S C5  W H2
      note: Nothing left to win. Give up the rest.
  11  E HQ  S CJ  W H8  N H6
  12  E HT  S CQ  W HJ  N C6
  13  W HA  N C7  E CT  S CK
      note: 9 tricks: 3NT made.

play recap:
  The plan: play dummy's ♥K at once, then take nine tricks without giving up
  the lead.

  The king won because West, who led, had the ace. Had you played low, East
  would have won with the ♥Q and led another heart, and West's ace would
  have captured the king. Then West would have run the hearts the moment you
  went after the clubs. Nine tricks were there all along, as long as the
  king went up at trick 1.
